at no one called him chalti phirti cocaine?", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-29T15:39:52Z", "username": "Chinmay Mehta", "text": "Next", "role": "elaboration", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-29T15:39:55Z", "username": "Lzafeer Ahmad B F", "text": "Next time some LinkedIn lunatic posts about bosses / founders doing grunt work must give this example", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-29T15:39:58Z", "username": "Yash Agrawal", "text": "Loved this @⁨~Smit D Mahajani⁩", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-29T15:40:01Z", "username": "Chinmay Mehta", "text": "15. 15. Whitey Burger was the undisputed king of South Boston's underworld for decades, but his most disturbing secret wasn't his criminal empire—it was his relationship with the state. Long before he became an FBI informant who evaded capture for 16 years, he was an inmate at Alcatraz in the 1950s. Looking for a way to reduce his sentence, he volunteered for what he thought was a medical study to find a cure for schizophrenia. In reality, he had unknowingly walked into MKUltra, a CIA mind-control program. Over the course of several months, he was subjected to over 50 \"trips\" using a specific chemical substance that he later claimed \"reprogrammed\" his brain, contributing to the cold, paranoid ruthlessness that allowed him to rule Boston. What was the CIA doing to him?", "role": "elaboration", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-29T15:40:09Z", "username": "Chinmay Mehta", "text": "Post this as inspiration", "role": "elaboration", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-29T15:40:13Z", "username": "Abhishek Saluja", "text": "Tapping phone", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": nul